  <h2>What substantial completion is, and what it is not</h2>
  <p>
    Substantial completion is the contractual point at which the work is sufficiently complete, in
    accordance with the contract documents, that the owner can occupy or use it for its intended
    purpose. It is not the last day on site. Minor items remain — the punch list — and the
    certificate says so on its face: it is not final completion, and signing it does not waive the
    owner's right to have the listed items corrected or later defects fixed under the warranty.
    Final completion comes when the punch list is done and the closeout package is accepted, and
    final payment follows that. The
    <a href="/blog/construction-project-closeout-checklist/">closeout checklist</a> walks through the
    whole sequence for a subcontractor.
  </p>

  <h2>Why one date carries so much</h2>
  <p>
    Four clocks usually start or stop on the date of substantial completion. The
    <strong>warranty period</strong> begins, so a twelve-month warranty is measured from here rather than
    from installation. <strong>Retainage</strong> release is triggered, in whole or in part, depending
    on the contract. <strong>Liquidated damages</strong>, where a contract has them, stop accruing.
    And <strong>responsibility for the work</strong> — utilities, security, heating and cooling, insurance,
    damage — passes to the owner, which is why the certificate has a checklist for exactly that transfer
    and a line for the responsibilities the contractor keeps (the punch list, and damage its own crews
    cause while finishing it). In some states the date also starts lien and bond claim deadlines. A certificate
    that both parties sign turns a date that would otherwise be argued about into a fact.
  </p>

  <h2>The punch list and the money</h2>
  <p>
    The certificate carries three numbers about the punch list: how many items, what the remaining
    work is worth, and when it will be done — and one rule: missing that date does not move the date
    of substantial completion. The retainage section then shows the arithmetic in the open:
    retainage held to date, the amount released with this certificate, the amount withheld until
    final completion, and the basis for the withholding. Many contracts let the owner or GC hold a
    multiple of the punch list value, commonly 150%, and release the rest; others hold everything to
    final completion; several states set the rule by statute. Whatever your agreement says, the
    three fields should foot, and the withheld amount should be traceable to the list. Attach the
    list itself — the <a href="/templates/punch-list/">punch list template</a> is built for it, and the
    <a href="/blog/punch-list-management-for-subcontractors/">punch list management guide</a> covers working
    it down to zero — and bill the released amount on the next <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/"
      >pay application</a
    >
    with the <a href="/templates/lien-waiver/">lien waiver</a> the contract requires.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is CSC-001 from Summit Mechanical Contractors, certifying the mechanical
    and plumbing scope of the Harbor Point Medical Office, Building B, substantially complete on
    November 18, 2026 — the completion date in the subcontract and the notice to proceed. The
    contract sum as adjusted is $497,354.50: the original $486,200.00 plus CO-001 (+$7,350.00),
    CO-002 (−$1,980.00) and CO-003 (+$5,784.50). Retainage held at 10% is $49,735.45. The punch list
    has 14 items worth $4,200.00, due December 4; 150% of that value, $6,300.00, is withheld until
    final completion and $43,435.45 is released with the certificate. The warranty runs to November
    18, 2027. Utilities, security, maintenance, heating and cooling, insurance and damage pass to
    the owner as of the date, with the contractor keeping the punch list and damage caused by its
    own crews. Record drawings, O&amp;M manuals, warranties, training and permit finals are
    delivered; attic stock is not. Summit certifies on November 18, Brightline Builders accepts on
    November 19, and Kestrel Design Group confirms the inspection.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Certificate of substantial completion vs. certificate of occupancy vs. letter of completion
  </h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Certificate of substantial completion</strong> — a contract document between the parties:
      the work has reached the stage the contract defines, with these items remaining.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Certificate of occupancy</strong> — issued by the building authority after final inspections;
      says the building may be legally occupied. Neither implies the other.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Letter of completion</strong> — a contractor's one-sided statement that the work is done,
      often used on small jobs. Useful as notice; weaker than a certificate both parties sign, because
      nothing in it is accepted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Final completion / closeout</strong> — punch list corrected, documents accepted, final payment
      made. The <a href="/templates/notice-to-proceed/">notice to proceed</a> opened the contract time;
      final completion closes the contract.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Letting the owner occupy the space without a certificate, then arguing about the date.</li>
    <li>
      Signing a certificate with no punch list attached, so the "minor items" have no boundary.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leaving the retainage fields blank and finding out at the final pay application that the two
      sides had different numbers in mind.
    </li>
    <li>
      Forgetting the warranty end date, so callbacks a year later start with a calendar argument.
    </li>
    <li>
      Not recording which responsibilities transferred — the heating bill and the broken window in
      week two both become disputes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Signing a final unconditional lien waiver at this milestone instead of with final payment.
    </li>
  </ul>
